<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus, <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(X)\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(X)\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore it suffices to prove (<a href="#S1.E8" title="In Theorem 5. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) with <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o(1)\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o(1)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Proofs of main results ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. First we use the second property of Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, which is an exponential tail bound, in order to control the <span id="S1.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">entropy tails</span>. We use the third property, which is a type of smoothness, to control the error term arising from the approximation of the density of <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}{X_{i}+U_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}{X_{i}+U_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> by the p.m.f. of <math id="S1.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}{X_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}{X_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combined with the Taylor-type estimate of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem19" title="Lemma 19 ([]). ‣ 2 Proofs of main results ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>, which is analogous to an estimate used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, this approximation is the main technical step in the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. The first property of Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> ensures that Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem19" title="Lemma 19 ([]). ‣ 2 Proofs of main results ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> is applicable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we will show that the assumption that the p.m.f. of <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies these three properties is weaker than almost isotropic log-concavity in the sense that any almost isotropic log-concave p.m.f. on <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies these properties with appropriate constants. We remark here that, since our definition of log-concavity reduces to the usual one for the one-dimensional case, Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> are also relaxed versions of the corresponding results obtained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. We prove the following theorem, which allows us to establish that if <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave with almost isotropic extension then <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the class <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\left(c_{1}(d),c_{2}(d),c_{3}(d)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\left(c_{1}(d),c_{2}(d),c_{3}(d)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> for some absolute constants <math id="S1.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(d),c_{2}(d),c_{3}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(d),c_{2}(d),c_{3}(d)</annotation></semantics></math> that depend on the dimension only (in particular, this theorem is essential for proving item <a href="#S3.I1.i1" title="item 1 ‣ Theorem 20. ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem20" title="Theorem 20. ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a log-concave p.m.f. on <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with almost isotropic extension and covariance matrix <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists an absolute constant <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (independent of the dimension), such that</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}{p(k)}\leq\frac{C^{\prime}}{\det\Bigl(\mathrm{Cov}_{\mathbb{Z}^{d}}(p)\Bigr)^{\frac{1}{2}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}{p(k)}\leq\frac{C^{\prime}}{\det\Bigl(\mathrm{Cov}_{\mathbb{Z}^{d}}(p)\Bigr)^{\frac{1}{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">provided that <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det\left(\mathrm{Cov}_{\mathbb{Z}^{d}}(p)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det\left(\mathrm{Cov}_{\mathbb{Z}^{d}}(p)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is large enough depending on <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> can be seen as <math id="S1.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional analogue of the following result, due to Bobkov, Marsiglietti and Melbourne <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, who also studied discrete versions of the EPI (up to multiplicative constants) for Rényi entropies of log-concave distributions and which is an important tool in the one-dimensional case:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Theorem 1.1]</span></cite><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
If a random variable <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> follows a discrete log-concave p.m.f. <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S1.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}f(k)\leq\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+4\sigma^{2}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k\in\mathbb{Z}}f(k)\leq\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+4\sigma^{2}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=\mathrm{Var}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=\mathrm{Var}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> may also be seen as a discrete analogue of a dimensional upper bound on the isotropic constant <math id="S1.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding (continuous) lower bound on <math id="S1.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> can be deduced from (<a href="#S1.E10" title="In Remark 6. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) and the fact that <math id="S1.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{-h(X)}\leq\max(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-h(X)}\leq\max(f)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}=\left(\max_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}{f}\right)^{\frac{1}{d}}\mathrm{det}(\mathrm{Cov}(f))^{\frac{1}{2d}}\geq e^{-\frac{h(X)}{d}}\mathrm{det}(\mathrm{Cov}(f))^{\frac{1}{2d}}\geq\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi e}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>det</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>det</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}=\left(\max_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}{f}\right)^{\frac{1}{d}}\mathrm{det}(\mathrm{Cov}(f))^{\frac{1}{2d}}\geq e^{-\frac{h(X)}{d}}\mathrm{det}(\mathrm{Cov}(f))^{\frac{1}{2d}}\geq\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi e}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p10.2" class="ltx_p">It is well known that a dimensional upper bound also exists and we denote by <math id="S1.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> the maximum of the isotropic constants <math id="S1.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> among log-concave functions <math id="S1.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The latter is related to the famous hyperplane (or slicing) problem in convex geometry, a longstanding conjecture recently solved in a major breakthrough by Klartag and Lehec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, following decisive progress by Guan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. A second, independent proof was subsequently given by Bizeul <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. The hyperplane and isotropic constant theorems, formerly known as conjectures, are in fact equivalent, as shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorem 3.1.2]</cite>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Theorem 1.1]</span></cite><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">

There exists a universal constant <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for any dimension <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, any convex body <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in isotropic position and of volume <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and any direction <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\mathbb{S}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</span></p>
<table id="S1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|K\cap\theta^{\perp}|_{d-1}\geq c." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|K\cap\theta^{\perp}|_{d-1}\geq c.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently, there exists a universal constant <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{d}\leq C." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{d}\leq C.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Henceforth, we will be referring to (<a href="#S1.E12" title="In Theorem 9. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> as the <span id="S1.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hyperplane Theorem</span> and to (<a href="#S1.E13" title="In Theorem 9. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) as the <span id="S1.SS2.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Isotropic Constant Theorem</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Our method for proving Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> is to use the corresponding continuous result.
To this end, we obtain the approximations</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Bigl|\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}{f}-\sum_{\mathbb{Z}^{d}}{f}\Bigr|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></munder></mstyle><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Bigl|\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}{f}-\sum_{\mathbb{Z}^{d}}{f}\Bigr|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
