<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Bigl|\det\Bigl(\mathrm{Cov}_{\mathbb{Z}^{d}}{(f)}\Bigr)-\det\Bigl(\mathrm{Cov}_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}{(f)}\Bigr)\Bigr|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Bigl|\det\Bigl(\mathrm{Cov}_{\mathbb{Z}^{d}}{(f)}\Bigr)-\det\Bigl(\mathrm{Cov}_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}{(f)}\Bigr)\Bigr|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=O_{d}(\sigma^{2d-1}),\quad\text{as }\sigma\to\infty," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>as </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=O_{d}(\sigma^{2d-1}),\quad\text{as }\sigma\to\infty,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p12.2" class="ltx_p">for any isotropic log-concave density <math id="S1.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. This is done in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Approximating the integral, mean and covariance discretely ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. Before that, after recalling some know facts about Ball’s bodies in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Ball’s bodies ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we prove the exponential tails bound in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A concentration lemma ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Although our results hold under the more general almost isotropicity assumption, for better illustration of the ideas, we assume first in Sections <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Ball’s bodies ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>—<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Approximating the integral, mean and covariance discretely ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> that the continuous extension <math id="S1.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is isotropic. In Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 Relaxing the isotropicity assumption on 𝑓 ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> we show how to relax this assumption (see Remark <a href="#Thmtheorem38" title="Remark 38. ‣ 3.5 Relaxing the isotropicity assumption on 𝑓 ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Concluding Remark and Open Question ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we conclude with a brief discussion of our assumptions as well as an open question.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Notations and definitions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we will detail the notations as well as key definitions. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Big- and small-<math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> notation. </span> Let <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> be a real-valued function and <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> another strictly positive function.
We write <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=O(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=O(g)</annotation></semantics></math> if there exist positive absolute constants <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,C</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f(x)|\leq Cg(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(x)|\leq Cg(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq N</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,C</annotation></semantics></math> are absolute up to a parameter <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=O_{d}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=O_{d}(g)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Analogously, we write <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\Omega(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\Omega(g)</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f(x)|\geq Cg(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(x)|\geq Cg(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq N</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\Omega(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\Omega(g)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=O(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=O(g)</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\Theta(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\Theta(g)</annotation></semantics></math> (with the analogous definitions for <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>).
When it is more convenient, we will write <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f\lesssim_{d}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≲</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\lesssim_{d}g</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=O_{d}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=O_{d}(g)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f\simeq_{d}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≃</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\simeq_{d}g</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\Theta(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\Theta(g)</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=o(g(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=o(g(x))</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convex bodies.</span> A <span id="S1.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">convex body</span> is a convex set that is compact and has a non-empty interior. For a convex body <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, one denotes by <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|K|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|K|</annotation></semantics></math> its volume. The <span id="S1.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">convex hull</span> of a set <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{conv(A)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{conv(A)}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the smallest convex set containing the set <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Log-concavity and convexity in <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. </span>
For <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, there are more than one definitions of log-concavity that have been used in different contexts. We will use a quite general definition that implies several other notions of log-concavity, the one of <span id="S1.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">extensible log-concave functions</span>. The reader is referred to Murota <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> for an extensive discussion of discrete convexity in higher dimensions.
A function <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <span id="S1.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">convex-extensible</span> if there exists a convex function <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}(z)=f(z)\hskip 8.5359pt(\forall z\in\mathbb{Z}^{d})\hskip 2.84544pt." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.850em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}(z)=f(z)\hskip 8.5359pt(\forall z\in\mathbb{Z}^{d})\hskip 2.84544pt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We refer to <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S1.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">extension</span> of <math id="S1.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
Based on the above, we define</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Log-concave extensible functions)<span id="Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A function <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R_{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R_{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be log-concave extensible if there exists <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>
convex-extensible such that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(z)=e^{-V(z)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(z)=e^{-V(z)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Throughout when we say that <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave, we mean that <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave extensible. We say that a random vector <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with values in <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and p.m.f. <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave, if <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, we define a <math id="S1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-convex set (commonly referred in the literature as <span id="S1.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hole-free</span> set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>):</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmtheorem11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-convexity)<span id="Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A set <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-convex if and only if its indicator function <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{A}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{A}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise, is log-concave-extensible or equivalently if</p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathrm{conv}(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{d}\hskip 2.84544pt." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathrm{conv}(A)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{d}\hskip 2.84544pt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Notice that the support of a log-concave function <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-convex.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 12</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math> our definition of log-concavity is equivalent to the usual definition <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(k)^{2}\geq p(k-1)p(k+1),k\in\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(k)^{2}\geq p(k-1)p(k+1),k\in\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is preserved under convolution (e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>). However, for <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> log-concavity may not be preserved in general, as pointed out by Murota <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Example 3.15]</cite> by considering two log-concave distributions supported on
<math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}=\{(0,0),(1,1)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}=\{(0,0),(1,1)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}=\{(0,1),(1,0)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}=\{(0,1),(1,0)\}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. This is because the Minkowski sum <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}+S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}+S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-convex.
On the other hand, if <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}=S_{2}=S\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}=S_{2}=S\subset\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-convex, then <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S+S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S+S</annotation></semantics></math> is also <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-convex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Corollary 2.54]</cite>. In <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> this is not true: consider the Reeve tetrahedron <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{(0,0,0),(1,0,0),(0,1,0),(1,1,2)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{(0,0,0),(1,0,0),(0,1,0),(1,1,2)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the set <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>-convex, but <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S+S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S+S</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain the point <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and is thus not <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>-convex.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notion of isotropicity</span>. The isotropic constant of a function <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}:=\left(\frac{\max_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}{f}}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}{f}}\right)^{\frac{1}{d}}\mathrm{det}(\mathrm{Cov}(f))^{\frac{1}{2d}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>det</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}:=\left(\frac{\max_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}{f}}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}{f}}\right)^{\frac{1}{d}}\mathrm{det}(\mathrm{Cov}(f))^{\frac{1}{2d}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}{(f)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}{(f)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S1.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inertia</span> or <span id="S1.SS3.p6.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">covariance</span> matrix defined, for <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i,j\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i,j\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathrm{Cov}{(f)}]_{ij}:=\frac{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}x_{i}x_{j}f(x)dx}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(x)dx}-\frac{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}x_{i}f(x)dx\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}x_{j}f(x)dx}{\Bigl(\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(x)dx\Bigr)^{2}}\hskip 2.84544pt." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathrm{Cov}{(f)}]_{ij}:=\frac{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}x_{i}x_{j}f(x)dx}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(x)dx}-\frac{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}x_{i}f(x)dx\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}x_{j}f(x)dx}{\Bigl(\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(x)dx\Bigr)^{2}}\hskip 2.84544pt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.SS3.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isotropic</span> if <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}{(f)}=\sigma^{2}\mathrm{I}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}{(f)}=\sigma^{2}\mathrm{I}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma&gt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{I}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{I}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix. Let <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex body in <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then its covariance matrix is <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}(K):=\mathrm{Cov}(\mathds{1}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}(K):=\mathrm{Cov}(\mathds{1}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> and its isotropic constant is <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{K}:=L_{\mathds{1}_{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mi>K</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{K}:=L_{\mathds{1}_{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The convex body <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S1.SS3.p6.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isotropic</span> if <math id="S1.SS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathds{1}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{1}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is isotropic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, in the discrete case <math id="S1.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathbb{Z}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the covariance matrix <math id="S1.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>
by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathrm{Cov}{(p)}]_{ij}:=\frac{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}k_{i}k_{j}p(k)}{\sum_{k}{p(k)}}-\frac{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}k_{i}p(k)\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}k_{j}p(k)}{\Bigl(\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}p(k)\Bigr)^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathrm{Cov}{(p)}]_{ij}:=\frac{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}k_{i}k_{j}p(k)}{\sum_{k}{p(k)}}-\frac{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}k_{i}p(k)\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}k_{j}p(k)}{\Bigl(\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}p(k)\Bigr)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 13</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A family <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{\sigma}\}_{\sigma\in\mathbb{R}_{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{\sigma}\}_{\sigma\in\mathbb{R}_{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> of non-negative functions on <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">almost isotropic</span> if, as <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Cov}{(f_{\sigma})}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Cov}{(f_{\sigma})}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma^{2}+O(\sigma)\quad\text{for }i=j," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma^{2}+O(\sigma)\quad\text{for }i=j,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=O(\sigma)\quad\text{for }i\neq j." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=O(\sigma)\quad\text{for }i\neq j.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 14</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is straightforward to check that
the family <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{\sigma}\}_{\sigma\in\mathbb{R}_{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{\sigma}\}_{\sigma\in\mathbb{R}_{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> is almost isotropic if and only if</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathrm{Cov}{(f_{\sigma})}-\sigma^{2}\mathrm{I}_{d}||_{\rm op}=O(\sigma)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathrm{Cov}{(f_{\sigma})}-\sigma^{2}\mathrm{I}_{d}||_{\rm op}=O(\sigma),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||A||_{\rm op}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||A||_{\rm op}</annotation></semantics></math> is the operator norm.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We are interested in log-concave densities <math id="S1.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, for which <math id="S1.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det{\mathrm{Cov}{(f)}}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det{\mathrm{Cov}{(f)}}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, when we write that <math id="S1.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is almost isotropic, it is meant that <math id="S1.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> represents a family of densities with the underlying parameter <math id="S1.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:=\det{\mathrm{Cov}{(f)}}^{\frac{1}{2d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:=\det{\mathrm{Cov}{(f)}}^{\frac{1}{2d}}</annotation></semantics></math> growing large.
When its not clear from the context, we write <math id="S1.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}_{\mathbb{R}}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}_{\mathbb{R}}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}{{}_{\mathbb{Z}}(f)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}{{}_{\mathbb{Z}}(f)}</annotation></semantics></math> to distinguish between the continuous and discrete covariance matrix of <math id="S1.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Proofs of main results</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first observation is that the class <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(c_{1},c_{2},c_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(c_{1},c_{2},c_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is closed under self-convolution up to constants, as shown by the following proposition:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 15</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose
<math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathcal{C}(c_{1}(d),c_{2}(d),c_{3}(d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathcal{C}(c_{1}(d),c_{2}(d),c_{3}(d))</annotation></semantics></math> for some constants <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(d),c_{2}(d)\geq 1,c_{3}(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(d),c_{2}(d)\geq 1,c_{3}(d)</annotation></semantics></math> depending on the dimension only. Then the convolution of <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> with itself <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> times,
<math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{n}:=p*\stackrel{{\scriptstyle n}}{{\ldots}}*p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mover><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mi>n</mi></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n}:=p*\stackrel{{\scriptstyle n}}{{\ldots}}*p</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{n}\in\mathcal{C}({n^{\frac{d}{2}}}c_{1}(d),n^{\frac{d}{2}}c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n),\sqrt{n}c_{3}(d))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n}\in\mathcal{C}({n^{\frac{d}{2}}}c_{1}(d),n^{\frac{d}{2}}c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n),\sqrt{n}c_{3}(d))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some possibly different constant <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n)</annotation></semantics></math> that depends on the dimension and <math id="Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem15.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem15.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">That is, denoting <math id="Thmtheorem15.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:=\det{\Bigl(\mathrm{Cov}{(p)}}\Bigr)^{\frac{1}{2d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">det</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:=\det{\Bigl(\mathrm{Cov}{(p)}}\Bigr)^{\frac{1}{2d}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}{p_{n}(k)}\leq\frac{c_{1}(d)}{\sigma^{d}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}{p_{n}(k)}\leq\frac{c_{1}(d)}{\sigma^{d}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|k\|_{\infty}\geq c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n)\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|k\|_{\infty}\geq c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n)\sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{n}(k)\leq\frac{c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n)}{\sigma^{d}}e^{-\frac{\|k\|_{1}}{c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n)\sigma}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo mathsize="1.400em">,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n}(k)\leq\frac{c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n)}{\sigma^{d}}e^{-\frac{\|k\|_{1}}{c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n)\sigma}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where in fact we may take</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n)=\bigl(3\cdot 4^{d+1}c_{2}(d)\bigr)^{2^{n}-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}^{\prime}(d,n)=\bigl(3\cdot 4^{d+1}c_{2}(d)\bigr)^{2^{n}-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{d}{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}}{|p_{n}(k)-p_{n}(k-e_{i})|}\leq\frac{c_{3}(d)}{\sigma}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{d}{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}}}{|p_{n}(k)-p_{n}(k-e_{i})|}\leq\frac{c_{3}(d)}{\sigma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
