<p id="S2.p16.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p16.3.1" class="ltx_text">for some constants <math id="S2.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}_{d,n},c^{\prime}(d,n)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}_{d,n},c^{\prime}(d,n)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> large enough. Therefore, for <math id="S2.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> large enough <math id="S2.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S_{n}+U^{(n)}}\leq 1/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S_{n}+U^{(n)}}\leq 1/e</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S2.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is increasing. Moreover, using (<a href="#S2.E29" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ 2 Proofs of main results ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) and that <math id="S2.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|_{\infty}\leq\|x\|_{1}\leq\sqrt{d}\|x\|_{\infty},x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|_{\infty}\leq\|x\|_{1}\leq\sqrt{d}\|x\|_{\infty},x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have, for <math id="S2.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> large enough,</span></p>
