<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Concluding Remark and Open Question</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem20" title="Theorem 20. ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> and therefore for Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ 1.2 Main results and paper outline ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> as well, we have assumed that there exists a continuous extension <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, which is almost isotropic. We have then shown that <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is also almost isotropic in the discrete sense. It would be more natural to start with the assumption that the discrete p.m.f. is isotropic. However, in this case we would not be able to use the continuous toolkit to prove our concentration lemma in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 A concentration lemma ‣ 3 Discrete isotropic log-concave random vectors ‣ On the monotonicity of discrete entropy for log-concave random vectors on ℤ^𝑑" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Nevertheless, we suspect that if the discrete p.m.f. is isotropic, then there exists a continuous extension that is almost isotropic and therefore our results for discrete log-concave distributions would still hold under this assumption:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem40" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem40.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 40</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem40.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem40.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem40.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem40.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem40.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a log-concave random vector with p.m.f. <math id="Thmtheorem40.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem40.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="Thmtheorem40.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is almost isotropic. Is there a continuous log-concave extension of <math id="Thmtheorem40.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem40.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> which is almost isotropic?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Availability Statement:</span>
This study is based on theoretical research and does not involve any empirical data. As such, there are no data sets associated with this manuscript.</p>
</div>
